        "original_id": "5d8e4366-39e5-5e2b-8d5a-1e9a941efa4b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Polihale State Park, Niihau, Kauai, Hawaii, Keʻe Beach\n- Organizations: Nā Pali Coast State Park\n\nFacts:\n- Polihale State Park is a remote wild beach on the western side of the Hawaiian island of Kauai.\n- Polihale State Park is the westernmost publicly accessible area in Hawaii.\n- Niihau is a privately owned island that lies farther west than Polihale State Park.\n- Niihau is the westernmost and seventh largest inhabited island in Hawaii.\n- The Nā Pali coast extends southwest from Keʻe Beach to Polihale State Park.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "a07d963c-147d-5709-94ba-e1e71d932418",
        "text": "Page: Polihale State Park. Sentence: Polihale State Park is a remote wild beach on the western side of the Hawaiian island of Kauai.\nPage: Polihale State Park. Sentence: It is the most western publicly accessible area in Hawaii, although the privately owned island of Niihau is further west.\nPage: Niihau. Sentence: Niʻ ihau ( ; Hawaiian: ] ) is the westernmost and seventh largest inhabited island in Hawaiʻ i.\nPage: Nā Pali Coast State Park. Sentence: The Nā Pali coast itself extends southwest starting at Keʻ e Beach extending all the way to Polihale State Park.",
